Subject: [PATCH 50/50] arch/unix: place the Forth arena below 4 GiB, so an ihandle fits
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

TODO 18. `openbios-unix` had not reached its prompt since 2026-08-26 and nothing
noticed, because MANUAL_TESTING.md section 5 documented it and no track drove it.

    $ printf '3 4 + .\nbye\n' | obj-amd64/openbios-unix obj-amd64/openbios-unix.dict
    encode-int: value does not fit in the 4 bytes 1275 encodes an integer into
    $ echo $?
    0

THE REFUSAL IS CORRECT AND IS NOT TOUCHED HERE. That is the whole point of this
patch, and the trap it avoids. The gate patch 26 added looks mis-scoped -- it
sits in `l!-be`, a general four-byte big-endian store -- so the obvious move is
to narrow it to encode-int. Measured instead: ALL FIVE trips come through
`int!`, the 1275 property encoder, and none through a raw l!-be. Narrowing the
scope would have changed nothing, and weakening the gate would only have
restored the silent truncation patch 26 removed.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG is where the memory is. /chosen's `stdin` and `stdout`
are ihandles (forth/admin/iocontrol.fs:42,76); IEEE 1275 encodes an integer into
FOUR bytes (5.3.5.1); and at run time pointer2cell is a plain cast
(include/kernel/stack.h:35 -- Makefile.target defines
NATIVE_BITWIDTH_EQUALS_HOST_BITWIDTH for every target), so on this target an
ihandle IS a host pointer. glibc placed the 4 MiB arena above 4 GiB
(0x7a3ad9e03650 in one run) and encode-int rightly refused.

EVERY OTHER TARGET IS ALREADY WHERE THIS PUTS US. The QEMU firmwares live at
0x400000 and 0x4000000, so their pointers have always fitted in four bytes. This
does not make 1275 able to hold a 64-bit pointer; it stops this one target from
being the only place that asks it to. patch 48 records the same asymmetry from
the other side: at NARROWER widths cross.h subtracts base_address, which is why
arch/x86 never showed any of this.

    0 > stdin @ u. 20004ba8  ok        \ was above 4 GiB, and refused
    0 > start-mem @ u. 20000000  ok

AND IT REFUSES BY NAME IF IT CANNOT. A hint is only a hint, so the result is
checked and a mapping that still lands high panics and exits non-zero rather
than continuing into an abort that used to exit 0 (TODO 18(b)).

THE munmap IS PART OF THE FIX, not tidiness -- and the first version of this
patch got that wrong in a way worth recording. These two allocations are mmap
regions now, so the free()s that ended main() handed glibc pointers it never
allocated, and free() READS ITS CHUNK HEADER BELOW THE POINTER, at p-8. That one
mistake wore two costumes a page apart: "panic: segmentation violation at
1ffffff8" (arena base minus 8) while the page below was unmapped, and
"free(): invalid pointer" once something was mapped there for glibc to inspect.
The first draft mistook the SIGSEGV for a PRE-EXISTING firmware read and carried
a guard page to absorb it. There is no such read: with munmap here and NO guard
page the session runs clean, and a gdb access-watchpoint on memory-8 and -16
never fires across a whole session. The guard page is gone.

WHAT SUSTAINED THAT ERROR is the useful part. The panic dump prints
`pc=0x3000a0a0(dict+0xa0a0)`, which resolves offline to a point inside `bye` --
and that is the firmware's Forth PC GLOBAL, not the faulting instruction. `bye`
was simply the last Forth word to run before main() returned and called free().
`dstackcnt=-1` is stale for the same reason. A cached record read as if it
described the present moment.

Covered by `smoke-openbios.sh unix`, which asserts THE PROPERTY rather than the
boot -- stdin, stdout and start-mem read back from the prompt must each fit in
four bytes. Its control is the revert: put the arena back on the heap and the
track fails by name ("never printed its banner, so initialisation did not
complete").

diff --git a/arch/unix/unix.c b/arch/unix/unix.c
index cb9757e..79dcce0 100644
--- a/arch/unix/unix.c
+++ b/arch/unix/unix.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <termios.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
+#include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <stdarg.h>
 
 #ifdef __GLIBC__
@@ -363,13 +364,72 @@ int_handler(int signo __attribute__ ((unused)),
     exit(1);
 }
 
+/* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 18.
+ *
+ * PLACE THE FORTH ARENA AND THE DICTIONARY BELOW 4 GiB.
+ *
+ * 1275 encodes an integer into FOUR bytes (5.3.5.1), and /chosen's `stdin` and
+ * `stdout` are ihandles -- pointers into these two allocations
+ * (forth/admin/iocontrol.fs:42,76). At run time pointer2cell is a plain cast
+ * (include/kernel/stack.h:35; Makefile.target defines
+ * NATIVE_BITWIDTH_EQUALS_HOST_BITWIDTH for every target), so an ihandle IS a
+ * host pointer. glibc hands these two sizes out well above 4 GiB on a 64-bit
+ * host, so encode-int refused -- correctly -- and the firmware halted during
+ * initialisation, which is what MANUAL_TESTING.md section 5 recorded.
+ *
+ * THIS DOES NOT MAKE 1275 ABLE TO HOLD A 64-BIT POINTER, and it is not a
+ * weakening of patch 26's gate, which stays exactly as it is. It puts this
+ * target where every other target has always been: the QEMU firmwares live at
+ * 0x400000 and 0x4000000, so their pointers have always fitted in four bytes.
+ *
+ * AND IT REFUSES BY NAME RATHER THAN DRIFTING BACK. If the low mapping cannot
+ * be had, we say so and exit non-zero instead of continuing into an abort that
+ * used to exit 0 (TODO 18(b)) -- an honest failure the caller can see.
+ */
+#define OF_LOW_LIMIT   ((uintptr_t)1 << 32)
+
+static void *alloc_below_4g(size_t len, uintptr_t hint, const char *what)
+{
+	void *p = mmap((void *)hint, len, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
+	               MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
+
+	if (p == MAP_FAILED)
+		return NULL;
+
+	/* A hint is only a hint: verify, never assume. */
+	if ((uintptr_t)p + len > OF_LOW_LIMIT) {
+		printk("panic: %s landed at %p, above 4 GiB -- an ihandle there "
+		       "cannot be encoded into the four bytes IEEE 1275 gives an "
+		       "integer (5.3.5.1). See TODO 18.\n", what, p);
+		munmap(p, len);
+		return NULL;
+	}
+	return p;
+}
+
+/* The matching free, and it is load-bearing rather than tidiness.
+ *
+ * These two are mmap'd now, so the free()s that used to end main() hand glibc a
+ * pointer it never allocated -- and free() reads its chunk header BELOW the
+ * pointer, at p-8. That read is what produced "panic: segmentation violation at
+ * 1ffffff8" (arena base minus 8) while the page below was unmapped, and
+ * "free(): invalid pointer" once it was mapped and glibc got to inspect the
+ * garbage it found. One bug, two costumes. Measured: with munmap here and NO
+ * guard page, the session runs clean, and a gdb access-watchpoint on
+ * memory-8/-16 never fires across a whole session. */
+static void free_below_4g(void *p, size_t len)
+{
+	if (p)
+		munmap(p, len);
+}
+
 /*
  * allocate memory and prepare engine for memory management.
  */
 
 static void init_memory(void)
 {
-	memory = malloc(MEMORY_SIZE);
+	memory = alloc_below_4g(MEMORY_SIZE, 0x20000000, "the Forth arena");
 	if (!memory) {
 		printk("panic: not enough memory on host system.\n");
 		exit_terminal();
@@ -521,7 +581,9 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 	/* Initialise console */
 	init_console(unix_console_ops);
 
-	if ((dict = (unsigned char *) malloc(DICTIONARY_SIZE)) == NULL) {
+	if ((dict = (unsigned char *) alloc_below_4g(DICTIONARY_SIZE,
+	                                            0x30000000,
+	                                            "the dictionary")) == NULL) {
 		printk("panic: not enough memory.\n");
 		return 1;
 	}
@@ -585,7 +647,7 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 			write_dictionary(dictname);
 #endif
 
-		free(memory);
+		free_below_4g(memory, MEMORY_SIZE);
 
 	} else {		/* input file is not a dictionary */
 		printk("not supported.\n");
@@ -595,7 +657,7 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 	if (diskemu!=-1)
 		close(diskemu);
 
-	free(dict);
+	free_below_4g(dict, DICTIONARY_SIZE);
 	return 0;
 }
 
